</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:4.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">There is More to Attention: Statistical Filtering Enhances Explanations in Vision Transformers: 
<br class="ltx_break">Supplementary Materials</span></p>
</div><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>METRICS</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Among the different ways of evaluating an XAI method, we draw inspiration from  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu-Darme et al.(2023)</a>]</cite> to evaluate the <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Correctness</span>, <span id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Plausibility</span> and <span id="A1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stability</span> of the explanation maps. Correctness (faithfulness) quantifies the degree to which an explanation reflects the model’s internal decision-making. Plausibility (coherency) assesses whether the explanation is meaningful and interpretable from a human perspective. Stability (sensitivity) measures the consistency of explanations, evaluating whether similar inputs yield similar explanation maps.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Correctness</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deletion Area Under Curve (DAUC)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petsiuk et al.(2018)</a>]</cite>: It iteratively masks out the important regions of an input image, identified by the explanation map <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, from the most to the least important pixels. It then measures the variations in the model output with these masked inputs and the area under this curve gives the DAUC curve. In our experiments, we use a step size of 50 to uniformly mask pixels, progressing from no masking to complete masking. For a faithful explanation method, we expect a rapid drop in the model output as the most important regions are masked, resulting in a lower DAUC value.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insertion Area Under Curve (IAUC)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petsiuk et al.(2018)</a>]</cite>:This metric complements DAUC by starting with an empty image and progressively adding pixels based on the importance scores from the explanation map <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. For a faithful explanation method, the model output should increase rapidly as the most important regions are added, resulting in a higher IAUC value.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Drop (AD)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chattopadhay et al.(2018)</a>]</cite>: It measures the average decrease in model confidence when the input image is masked by the explanation map <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. A lower AD indicates a more faithful saliency map as given by Equation (<a href="#A1.E8" title="In 3rd item ‣ A.1 Correctness ‣ Appendix A METRICS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), where <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the original model output for a class <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{c}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{c}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the confidence for the masked input.</p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AD}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{max(0,p_{c}^{i}-o_{c}^{i})}{p_{c}^{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AD</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AD}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{max(0,p_{c}^{i}-o_{c}^{i})}{p_{c}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Increase (AI)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chattopadhay et al.(2018)</a>]</cite>: It measures the fraction of test samples for which the model’s confidence increases when it is only given the input masked by the explanation map as given by Equation (<a href="#A1.E9" title="In 4th item ‣ A.1 Correctness ‣ Appendix A METRICS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). A higher AI suggest that the explanation method highlights informative regions.</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AI}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}\cdot(o_{c}^{i}&gt;p_{c}^{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AI</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AI}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbbm{1}\cdot(o_{c}^{i}&gt;p_{c}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Gain (AG)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al.(2024)</a>]</cite>: It is the symmetric counterpart for AD and measures the gain in class probability after masking the input with the explanation map as given by Equation (<a href="#A1.E10" title="In 5th item ‣ A.1 Correctness ‣ Appendix A METRICS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). A higher AG indicates that the XAI method identifies important regions and also enhances the <span id="A1.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">certainty</span> of the model.</p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AG}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{max(0,o_{c}^{i}-p_{c}^{i})}{1-p_{c}^{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AG</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AG}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{max(0,o_{c}^{i}-p_{c}^{i})}{1-p_{c}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There exist multiple ways to measure perturbation-based faithfulness. Recent works introduced refined metrics to better account for out-of-distribution effects or baseline sensitivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samek et al.(2016)</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bluecher et al.(2024)</a>]</cite>. DAUC and IAUC  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petsiuk et al.(2018)</a>]</cite> remain widely adopted benchmarks, measuring how model output changes as pixels are progressively added to or removed from the image based on the saliency rankings. Recent evaluations extend this by considering both most relevant first (MoRF) and least relevant first (LeRF) perturbations orders. Combined with deletion (also called flipping (F)) and insertion (I), this yields four AUC metrics: <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{F}_{\text{MoRF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mtext>MoRF</mtext><mi>F</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{F}_{\text{MoRF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{F}_{\text{LeRF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mtext>LeRF</mtext><mi>F</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{F}_{\text{LeRF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{I}_{\text{MoRF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mtext>MoRF</mtext><mi>I</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{I}_{\text{MoRF}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{I}_{\text{LeRF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mtext>LeRF</mtext><mi>I</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{I}_{\text{LeRF}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achtibat et al.(2024)</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bluecher et al.(2024)</a>]</cite>. To further enhance robustness, the <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A</annotation></semantics></math> score is often used, which is computed as the difference between area under the LeRF and MoRF curves, offering a more stable measure of explanation quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Plausibility</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we compare explanation maps generated by different XAI methods against the GFDMs provided with the datasets, which we treat as ground truth. The plausibility of an explanation map is defined as its correspondence with human visual perception of the content. The closer an XAI map is to the GFDM for a given image, the more effective the sample-based explainer is. For this comparison, we employ established saliency and explanation map evaluation metrics, as suggested in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Shen(2017)</a>]</cite>. Since XAI maps are ultimately intended for human interpretation, we argue that plausibility metrics should take precedence over alternative evaluation criteria, as they directly assess human interpretability while still providing insight into the model’s decision-making process.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Similarity (Sim)</span>: It measures the similarity between the GFDM maps <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and the explanation map <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by treating them as discrete probability distributions, as defined in Equation (<a href="#A1.E11" title="In 1st item ‣ A.2 Plausibility ‣ Appendix A METRICS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). A SIM value of 0 indicates no overlap between the distributions, whereas a SIM value of 1 indicates identical distributions.</p>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathit{SIM}(S,G)=\sum_{i}\mathit{min}(S^{{}^{\prime}}_{i},G^{{}^{\prime}}_{i})\quad\text{where}\quad\sum_{i}S^{\prime}_{i}=1\quad\text{and}\quad\sum_{i}G^{\prime}_{i}=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐼𝑀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mi>𝑚𝑖𝑛</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>where</mtext><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>and</mtext><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SIM}(S,G)=\sum_{i}\mathit{min}(S^{{}^{\prime}}_{i},G^{{}^{\prime}}_{i})\quad\text{where}\quad\sum_{i}S^{\prime}_{i}=1\quad\text{and}\quad\sum_{i}G^{\prime}_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC)</span>: It measures the strength and direction of the linear relationship between the GFDM <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and the explanation <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, treated as random variables, as shown in Equation (<a href="#A1.E12" title="In 2nd item ‣ A.2 Plausibility ‣ Appendix A METRICS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Here, <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cov}(S,G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cov}(S,G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the covariance of <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are the standard deviations. PCC of 1 indicates perfect correlation, 0 indicates no correlation and a value close to -1 indicates strong negative correlation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{PCC}(S,G)=\frac{\mathrm{cov}(S,G)}{\sigma_{S}\times\sigma_{G}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑃𝐶𝐶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{PCC}(S,G)=\frac{\mathrm{cov}(S,G)}{\sigma_{S}\times\sigma_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUC-Judd</span>: This metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Shen(2017)</a>]</cite> computes the area under the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve and is widely adopted for evaluating saliency maps. Given a GFDM map
<math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, fixation locations are defined by thresholding <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a binary fixation map <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, where pixels with fixation values are marked as positives. The explanation map
<math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is then treated as a prediction map, and each pixel is classified as fixation or non-fixation with respect to
<math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. By varying a decision threshold over the continuous values of
<math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> from 0 to 1, one can compute the corresponding true positive rate (TPR) and false positive rate (FPR), thereby generating the ROC curve. The AUC-Judd score is defined as the area under this curve, with higher values indicating stronger alignment between the explanation map and human fixation patterns. We select the top-5% of the pixels from the GFDM <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normalized Scanpath Saliency (NSS)</span>: Similar to AUC, NSS also first creates the map of fixations <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> by thresholding <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total fixation points in <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
It measures how strong the saliency is at the ”important” fixation locations, as given in Equation (<a href="#A1.E13" title="In 4th item ‣ A.2 Plausibility ‣ Appendix A METRICS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), where <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu(.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(.)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma(.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(.)</annotation></semantics></math> are mean and standard deviation respectively</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.I2.i4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{NSS}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bar{S}\times F(i),\text{ where }\bar{S}=\frac{S-\mu(S)}{\sigma(S)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>NSS</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{NSS}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bar{S}\times F(i),\text{ where }\bar{S}=\frac{S-\mu(S)}{\sigma(S)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Stability</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Understanding the stability of explanation methods is critical when evaluating their reliability. XAI should produce similar explanations for similar inputs, especially in the local neighbourhood of a given input sample.</p>
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lipschitz criterion (LIP)</span>: The LIP metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alvarez Melis and Jaakkola(2018)</a>]</cite> draws inspiration from Lipschitz continuity criterion to measure the local stability of the explanation method. It measures the stability of the method, by evaluating the maximum ratio of change in explanations to the change in inputs within a small radius <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in Equation (<a href="#A1.E14" title="In 1st item ‣ A.3 Stability ‣ Appendix A METRICS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Here <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is original input, <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the perturbed version of the input and <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the explanation map for an input <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LIP(X_{0})=\text{max}_{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}&lt;\epsilon}\frac{\|S_{X_{0}}-S_{\widetilde{X}}\|_{2}}{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>max</mtext><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LIP(X_{0})=\text{max}_{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}&lt;\epsilon}\frac{\|S_{X_{0}}-S_{\widetilde{X}}\|_{2}}{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, this metric does not take into account the underlying model behaviour as an unstable model output for <math id="A1.I3.i1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> would result in a corresponding unstable <math id="A1.I3.i1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{X_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{X_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> which should be a correct explanation but LIP would penalise this. Thus,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu-Darme et al.(2023)</a>]</cite> propose the Local Surrogate Stability (LSS) metric.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local Surrogate Stability (LSS)</span>: Unlike LIP, LSS incorporates model behaviour while measuring explanation stability under assumption that explanations should vary only when the model’s behaviour changes. To evaluate the local behaviour, a surrogate model is constructed from the explanation method as proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeh et al.(2019)</a>]</cite>. This surrogate serves as a first-order linear approximation of the model’s output, using the saliency map at <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and is defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{X}=S_{X_{0}}^{\top}\dot{(}X-X_{0})+g(X_{0})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{X}=S_{X_{0}}^{\top}\dot{(}X-X_{0})+g(X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{X_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{X_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the explanation for a sample <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is the output (logit) of the model for <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is surrogate model’s approximation of <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This approximation assumes that the model <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> behaves approximately linearly in a small neighbourhood of <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="A1.I3.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and its perturbed version <math id="A1.I3.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.I3.i2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.I3.i2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\frac{X_{0}+\widetilde{X}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\frac{X_{0}+\widetilde{X}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be their midpoint. The deviation in the explanation at this midpoint is defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(X_{0},\widetilde{X})=E_{X_{0}}(m)-E_{\widetilde{X}}(m)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(X_{0},\widetilde{X})=E_{X_{0}}(m)-E_{\widetilde{X}}(m)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then the LSS is measured as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LSS(X_{0})=\text{max}_{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}&lt;\epsilon}\frac{D(X_{0},\widetilde{X})}{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>max</mtext><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LSS(X_{0})=\text{max}_{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}&lt;\epsilon}\frac{D(X_{0},\widetilde{X})}{\|X_{0}-\widetilde{X}\|_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">LSS penalises explanations that change when the model’s behaviour does not and rewards explanation methods that only vary when the model’s output also varies.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are done using a ViT-B16 model with 12 transformer layers (<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=12</annotation></semantics></math>), 12 attention heads (<math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=12</annotation></semantics></math>), linear patch size of 16 (<math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=16</annotation></semantics></math>), and an input image resolution of <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math>. It is initialised with pretrained ImageNet1K weights. For the MexCulture dataset, we fine-tune the model for 20 epochs, with a batch size of 16, achieving a final test accuracy of <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="89.71\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>89.71</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">89.71\%</annotation></semantics></math>. For the SALICON dataset, the model is trained for 25 epochs with a batch size of 32 and reaches a final test accuracy of <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.84\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.84</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.84\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Both the networks were trained using the SGD optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e-4</annotation></semantics></math> and an exponential decay of <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e-4</annotation></semantics></math>. For experiments involving the ImageNet1K dataset, we use the pretrained model directly.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use implementation from the official repository<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://github.com/hila-chefer/Transformer-Explainability" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/hila-chefer/Transformer-Explainability</a></span></span></span> for the Attention rollout, Grad <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> Relevance and GradCAM methods and is under the MIT License. AttnLRP results were obtained using the lxt library from the authors<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://github.com/rachtibat/LRP-eXplains-Transformers" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/rachtibat/LRP-eXplains-Transformers</a></span></span></span> and has the The Clear BSD License. We implemented the code for SAW and our methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>ADDITIONAL EXPERIMENTS and RESULTS</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Effect of the <math id="A3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> Parameter in <math id="A3.SS1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-sigma rule</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">we evaluate the class-aware RFEM-Class method against its ablated variant, the class-agnostic RFEM. We analyze both methods across a range of threshold values <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=[-0.5,0,0.5,1,1.5,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=[-0.5,0,0.5,1,1.5,2]</annotation></semantics></math>, and include Rollout and SAW metrics as baselines, since RFEM and RFEM-Class are built from these respective approaches. The inclusion of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=-0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=-0.5</annotation></semantics></math> is to examine whether retaining attention values below the mean provides useful information. We used the MexCulture dataset and evaluate multiple metrics, including SIM, PCC, NSS, AUC, IAUC, DAUC, and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. As illustrated by Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.1 Effect of the 𝐾 Parameter in 𝐾-sigma rule ‣ Appendix C ADDITIONAL EXPERIMENTS and RESULTS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, RFEM-Class achieves higher perturbation scores (<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math>) and lower deletion scores (DAUC) compared to RFEM, highlighting the advantage of incorporating class-awareness for identifying more discriminative regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across most metrics, thresholding with a moderate <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in the range of <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> is better than Rollout and SAW and extreme values of <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. For both RFEM and RFEM-Class, SIM, PCC, NSS, and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math> generally peak in this range, indicating better alignment to ground truth, and improved localization of the salient regions. At very low (<math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=-0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=-0.5</annotation></semantics></math>) or high (<math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.0</annotation></semantics></math>) threshold values, performance degrades across all metrics, suggesting that overly lenient filtering introduces noise while aggressive thresholding discards essential information. When compared to Rollout and SAW, both RFEM and RFEM-Class with statistical filtering are better for both the <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and Deletion (DAUC) metrics, thus improving faithfulness. This demonstrates that our methods can enhance the quality and reliability of visual explanations beyond standard attention methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06070v1/ablation_RFEM_final.png" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:673/359;" width="673" height="359" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Effect of changing <math id="A3.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=[-0.5,0,0.5,1,1.5,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=[-0.5,0,0.5,1,1.5,2]</annotation></semantics></math> for our methods RFEM and RFEM-Class for the XAI metrics : SIM, PCC, NSS, IAUC, DAUC and <math id="A3.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on the MexCulture dataset. The dotted lines represent the corresponding metric values of the baseline methods, Rollout and SAW, from which RFEM and RFEM-Class are derived.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="A3.F8.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A3.F8.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.F8.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">
<div id="A3.F8.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:8.9pt;height:40.7pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:40.7pt;transform:translate(-15.9pt,-15.9pt) rotate(-90deg) ;">
<p id="A3.F8.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.F8.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Original</span></p>
</span></div></th>
